window.VGRE_SEARCH_INDEX = [{"url": "index.html", "title": "Introduction", "section": "Overview", "text": "VGRE — Virtual GPU RuntimeRun unmodified CUDA on any CPU — no GPU required. Plus a full local-AI stack: train, fine-tune with LoRA, retrieve with an in-tree vector index, and generate — all offline, all dependency-free.Linux CI-greenmacOS CI-greenWindows CI-greenLLVM-free buildCUDA ~95%Zero vendor lock-inVGRE intercepts CUDA and OpenCL API calls and executes kernels on the CPU. It does not need LLVM to run kernels: a from-scratch CUDA-C front-end (own lexer/parser → AST) feeds a four-tier CPU backend — a PTX interpreter, a compiled-closure tier with a cooperative fiber executor (__shared__ / _"}, {"url": "quickstart.html", "title": "Quick Start", "section": "Overview", "text": "Quick StartFrom a one-line install to a running CUDA kernel on CPU in minutes.Fastest: install the prebuilt wheel (no toolchain)Each GitHub Release ships a self-contained, LLVM-free wheel per platform (Linux / macOS / Windows). It bundles the native engine, so you need nothing but Python 3.8+ and NumPy — no compiler, no CUDA, no GPU:# Grab the wheel for your platform from the latest release, then: pip install vgre-0.1.0-py3-none-linux_x86_64.whl # or -macosx_* / -win_amd64 python -c &quot;import vgre; print(&#x27;native:&#x27;, vgre.NATIVE_AVAILABLE)&quot;TipReleases: github.com/etienne0114/vg"}, {"url": "downloads.html", "title": "Downloads", "section": "Overview", "text": "DownloadsPrebuilt, self-contained wheels — no compiler, no CUDA, no GPU.Every GitHub Release ships a self-contained, LLVM-free wheel per platform. Each bundles the native engine, so you need only Python 3.8+ and NumPy. The wheels are built and smoke-tested (import + train a language model) on Linux, macOS and Windows runners in CI.Latest release — v0.1.0Pick your platform, download the wheel, then copy the pip install command (each block has a Copy button):🐧 Linux x86-64⬇ Download vgre-0.1.0-py3-none-linux_x86_64.whlpip install vgre-0.1.0-py3-none-linux_x86_64.whl🍎 macOS (Apple Silicon)⬇ Downl"}, {"url": "installation.html", "title": "Installation", "section": "Overview", "text": "InstallationManual build steps and platform notes.TipThe quickest way to get running is the prebuilt wheel — see Downloads. Build from source when you want the dashboard, the cluster tools, or to hack on the engine.PrerequisitesLinux (Ubuntu / Debian)# Full build (with the optional LLVM JIT): sudo apt install -y cmake ninja-build clang llvm-18-dev \\ libomp-dev libssl-dev libsqlite3-dev libkeyutils-dev zlib1g-dev # LLVM-free build needs no llvm-18-dev / libomp-dev — just clang + the above.macOS (Homebrew — paths discovered automatically at configure time)brew install cmake llvm@18 libomp ninja "}, {"url": "running-cuda.html", "title": "Running CUDA Apps", "section": "Overview", "text": "Running CUDA ApplicationsHow VGRE intercepts and executes CUDA on the CPU.Execution modelVGRE parses each CUDA-C kernel with its own from-scratch front-end (no Clang required) and runs it on the fastest of four bit-exact CPU tiers that accepts it: a PTX interpreter, a compiled-closure tier with a cooperative fiber executor, a hand-emitted native x86-64 JIT, and an optional SSA optimizing backend. When LLVM dev libraries are present, an optional Clang → LLVM IR → ORC-JIT path (-O3 -march=native) is available as the high-performance tier. Kernel grids run on an in-tree work-stealing thread pool "}, {"url": "cluster.html", "title": "Distributed Clusters", "section": "Overview", "text": "Distributed ClustersScale kernels across machines over LAN or WAN.Token setupEvery node in a cluster shares one auth token. Generate it on the master and distribute it to workers:# On the MASTER vgre-token generate # writes ~/.vgre/token, prints SHA-256 fingerprint # On every WORKER (choose one) scp master:~/.vgre/token ~/.vgre/token vgre-token set &lt;paste-token-here&gt; # Verify both sides match vgre-token fingerprintStart the cluster# Master (LAN auto-discovery on) vgre-start --master # Workers on the same LAN vgre-start --worker # Workers over WAN (explicit master address) vgre-start --wo"}, {"url": "dashboard.html", "title": "The Dashboard", "section": "Overview", "text": "The DashboardA real-time Flutter monitor for the running engine.Launch with vgre-dashboard. It loads the native library in-process and polls live telemetry — no mock data. Pages:PageWhat it showsDashboardLive utilization gauges, workload throughput, UVM activity, log stream.Kernel ExplorerJIT-compiled kernels, launch counts, occupancy, per-kernel timing.Cluster TopologyConnected nodes, per-node load, network heatmap.Hardware TuningToggle background compute, block-threads, profiler; set cache model.Memory AnalysisAllocation map, bandwidth stats, UVM page residency.SettingsPolling interval, data"}, {"url": "local-ai.html", "title": "Local AI: LoRA · RAG · LM", "section": "Overview", "text": "Local AI: LoRA · RAG · LMA complete offline machine-learning stack built into the runtime — no PyTorch, no FAISS, no external tokenizer library.LoRA fine-tuningvgre.nn.LoRALinear adds trainable low-rank adapters over a frozen base weight, so you can fine-tune on a CPU/laptop with a fraction of the parameters. Adapters merge back to the base for zero-overhead inference.from vgre import nn layer = nn.LoRALinear.from_linear(pretrained, r=8, alpha=16) opt = nn.AdamW(layer.adapter_parameters(), lr=1e-3) # trains A,B only # ... training loop ... layer.merge() # fold adapter into W for fast inference"}, {"url": "debugging.html", "title": "Debugging PTX (GDB)", "section": "Overview", "text": "Debugging PTX with GDBCUDA-GDB-style single-stepping through PTX kernels using a stock gdb — no GPU, no cuda-gdb install.How it worksVGRE ships a PTX single-step interpreter and a GDB remote-serial-protocol server. Each CUDA thread appears as a GDB thread (a lane); the kernel's own virtual PTX registers are exposed to info registers; and breakpoints, stepi, and memory inspection all behave normally in an unmodified gdb.Start the stubvgre_ptx_gdbserver kernel.ptx saxpy 1 4 2000 \\ f32:2.0 buf:f32:4=1,2,3,4 buf:f32:4=10,20,30,40 u32:4 # prints the instruction listing, buffer addresses, and: # gdb"}, {"url": "env-vars.html", "title": "Environment Variables", "section": "Overview", "text": "Environment VariablesWritten to ~/.vgre/env by the installer and loaded automatically.CoreVariableDefaultDescriptionVGRE_LIB_PATHautoAbsolute path to the shared library.VGRE_LOG_LEVELINFODEBUG | INFO | WARN | ERROR.VGRE_INSTALL_DIR~/.local/share/VGREInstallation directory.VGRE_WORKER_THREADSnprocOverride worker thread count.VGRE_SIMD_LEVELautoForce SSE4 | AVX | AVX2 | AVX512.VGRE_ENABLE_NUMA0NUMA-aware scheduling (Linux).Execution backendVariableDefaultDescriptionVGRE_EXEC_BACKENDautoSelect the execution tier: interp (PTX interpreter), cp (compiled-fiber), or ssa (the Tier-2 SSA optimizing bac"}, {"url": "api.html", "title": "API Reference", "section": "Overview", "text": "API ReferencePython, C ABI, and the CUDA-runtime shim.Python bindingsThe vgre package wraps the native library via ctypes:SymbolPurposevgre.TokenizerBPE tokenizer; train, load_hf, encode, decode.vgre.LanguageModelIn-tree GPT; train, generate, save, load.vgre.nn.LoRALinearLow-rank adapters; adapter_parameters, merge.vgre.vector.VectorIndexHNSW ANN; add, search, save, load.C ABI (vgre_c_api.h)The stable C surface the wheel and dashboard bind to. Highlights:vgre_init(); vgre_set_profiler_enabled(1); vgre_set_block_threads(0); vgre_get_memory_bandwidth_stats(&amp;stats); // tokenizer vgre_bpe* t ="}, {"url": "cli.html", "title": "CLI Tools", "section": "Overview", "text": "CLI ToolsEvery command installed with VGRE, what it does, and how to use it.install_local.sh links the runtime tools into ~/.local/bin (add it to PATH — the installer does this for you). On Windows the same commands ship as .bat / .ps1 scripts (see the note at the bottom). Every command below prints usage with --help.Cluster &amp;amp; runtimeCommandWhat it doesCommon usagevgre-startStart a master or worker node (auto-sources ~/.vgre/env).vgre-start --master · --worker · --worker --master-ip 10.0.0.5 · --worker --port 7778 · --test (master+worker on one box)vgre-workerThe worker binary itself ("}, {"url": "architecture.html", "title": "Architecture", "section": "Overview", "text": "ArchitectureHow the pieces fit together.LayersInterception — CUDA/OpenCL API shims capture calls at load time (a drop-in libcudart preload, or the C ABI / Python bindings).Front-end — a from-scratch CUDA-C lexer/parser lowers kernels to an AST (no Clang required); an optional Clang/LLVM path is used when LLVM dev libs are present.Execution (four CPU tiers, fastest-first, bit-exact) — see the table below.Memory — UVM via an OS page-fault handler; stream-ordered pools; LZ4-compressed cluster transfers.Distribution — authenticated, encrypted TCP transport; NCCL-style + software-RDMA collectives; "}, {"url": "faq.html", "title": "FAQ & Troubleshooting", "section": "Overview", "text": "FAQ &amp; TroubleshootingDoes VGRE need a GPU?No. That is the point — kernels execute on the CPU. A GPU is never required.Is it as fast as a real GPU?No. Expect 10–50× slower for compute-bound kernels; memory-bound kernels are closer with AVX-512. VGRE targets learning, CI, development, and moderate workloads — not latency-critical production inference.Which platforms are supported?All three are CI-green. The full ctest suite passes on Linux x86-64 (394 tests with LLVM, 374 in the LLVM-free build — the required CI job), on macOS (Apple Silicon, auto-detected Homebrew llvm@18), and on Windows ("}];
